#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ff42d1-7d91-59db-9ac4-7c3a9e11dfe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.34)-Φ(-1.34)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516227} {:answer/id #uuid "6092f662-75ed-5f8a-9564-15a7e43b742f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.28)-Φ(-1.28)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516322} {:answer/id #uuid "cd7d0474-7aea-50f9-ae3c-3081a543503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.98)-Φ(-0.98)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516546} {:answer/id #uuid "cef03ff5-191a-5d70-bca6-cb830b7fda60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516552} {:answer/id #uuid "ffaee5fa-f5c5-56f1-b2fb-18822e27e724" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.72)-Φ(-0.72)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cef03ff5-191a-5d70-bca6-cb830b7fda60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516552} :answer-field/id #uuid "de7552ed-0a05-5263-8474-d257c2707a34" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186186378}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b040f12-bfb6-5271-9a10-66bca5c20f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-146163" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=100$ from a population where $60%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $55%$ and $65%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=100,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.6.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=100(0.6)=60>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=100(1-0.6)=40>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=100$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,\\frac{0.6(1-0.6)}{100}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.6,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.55\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.65) & =P(\\frac{0.55-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.65-0.6}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.02\\le Z\\le 1.02) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.02)-P(Z\\le -1.02) \\\\  & =Φ(1.02)-Φ(-1.02)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06171ae9-aab5-5555-8742-22098a666ee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.19)-Φ(-1.19)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516124} {:answer/id #uuid "076508d1-5160-580c-894f-c6ee9485bf2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(0.69)-Φ(-0.69)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516127} {:answer/id #uuid "54ade330-5c85-51a0-9b3d-21d097f4a0f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(2.26)-Φ(-2.26)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516290} {:answer/id #uuid "551a8f15-eac2-577e-8054-fb57a38b76a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.36)-Φ(-1.36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516292} {:answer/id #uuid "f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f918d5a6-7a95-5bf9-9d8e-e78ed7535f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516631} :answer-field/id #uuid "e66048ec-88b7-5ae4-8b5d-5521b6f747e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516755}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9faef3f5-2412-5ae3-81dd-9f520c533b8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-146937" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population where $70%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that between $60%$ and $80%$ of the sample have the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=80,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.7.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=80(0.7)=56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=80(1-0.7)=24>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=80$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,\\frac{0.7(1-0.7)}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.7,0.002\\,625).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.6\\le \\overset{^}{\\,p}\\le 0.8) & =P(\\frac{0.6-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}\\le Z\\le \\frac{0.8-0.7}{\\sqrt{0.002\\,625}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-1.95\\le Z\\le 1.95) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 1.95)-P(Z\\le -1.95) \\\\  & =Φ(1.95)-Φ(-1.95)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516769 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188259 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e7f6cfac-5f69-5c14-802b-433cb220f683" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Approximate Probability Using the CLT for Proportions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fce1eb7-6882-55e9-9a78-19b8b9ec12de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0565" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516439} {:answer/id #uuid "a11c1dd4-4d83-5f50-ab22-450ede146cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0428" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516443} {:answer/id #uuid "a4ee7691-eaaa-5a44-8e4b-9c9a62cb1212" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0615" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516455} {:answer/id #uuid "ddf18dde-67fe-5d37-ba3e-bc04e8232dc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0784" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516579} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0516" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2a58d8e-66ae-563e-a563-1b7fc88e1a4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0516" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516615} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4643a15-31bb-5228-8c72-0d5dcdaebd79" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516754}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "08394bc0-c2d2-5c3c-828d-b5a2969449ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-147070" :question/problem "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $36%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that less than $28%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.6\\,$ | $\\,0.0537\\,$ | $\\,0.0526\\,$ | $\\,0.0516\\,$ | $\\,0.0505\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0655\\,$ | $\\,0.0643\\,$ | $\\,0.0630\\,$ | $\\,0.0618\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ | $\\,0.0764\\,$ | $\\,0.0749\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Given a population in which a proportion $p$ of the population has a particular characteristic, the sampling distribution of the sample proportion $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,p}∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere the sample size $n$ is much smaller than the population size, $np>5,$ and $n(1-p)>5.$\n\nIn our case\n\n- the sample size is $n=96,$ and\n\n- the population proportion is $p=0.36.$\n\nNow, since\n\n$$\nnp=96(0.36)=34.56>5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nn(1-p)=96(1-0.36)=61.44>5,\n$$\n\nand $n=96$ is much smaller than the population size, we can apply the above approximation.\n\nThe sampling distribution of the sample proportion, in this case, can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\,p} & ∼N(p,\\frac{p(1-p)}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,\\frac{0.36(1-0.36)}{96}) \\\\  & ∼N(0.36,0.0024).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe probability we wish to find is\n$P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28).$\nSo, we convert $\\overset{^}{\\,p}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28) & =P(Z<\\frac{0.28-0.36}{\\sqrt{0.0024}}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.63) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.63).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.63) & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{^}{\\,p}<0.28) & =Φ(-1.63) \\\\  & =0.0516.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
